  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts a conventional wind turbine with a device labeled W-TENG, highlighting a shift from standard electromechanical generation to a layered triboelectric-style structure. On the left, the wind turbine is shown as a typical large tower system with rotor blades attached to a hub and nacelle. Inside the nacelle are familiar components such as a gearbox, brake, and generator, with additional control elements for blade pitch and yaw. The tower contains connecting wires and an access ladder, and the whole system is mounted on a basement, emphasizing the complexity and scale of the standard turbine architecture.\n\nOn the right, the W-TENG is presented as a much simpler, compact rectangular assembly. Its structure consists of stacked layers labeled substrate, electrodes, and dielectric material, held together by bolts at the ends. The image suggests a flat, layered energy-harvesting device rather than a rotating machine with many moving parts. Overall, the comparison visually emphasizes how a conventional wind turbine relies on bulky mechanical transmission and generator components, whereas the W-TENG appears to harvest wind energy through a simpler laminated material system with electrodes and dielectric layers.",
